speechtext:
I have not made
that suggestion yet. First we must realize that we have an industry which is worth cultivating and developing. In 1938 there were 19,593 acres in cultivation, producing 12-89 tons to the acre, compared with a United States average of 11-8 tons, in eleven states. California produced	11-5 tons;
Colorado, 13-7; Idaho. 13;	Utah, 13-5;
Nebraska, 14-and that was the highest. Those states have been producing beets for between thirty and forty years. The sugar content in Alberta was IS per cent as against 16 per cent in the United States. Only the state of California had as high a sugar content as Alberta. We produced 78,898,900 pounds of sugar. On November 7, 1938, over a million dollars was paid to the growers, at the rate of S5.35 a ton, representing the first payment. More payments were to follow as the sugar was sold.
I believe these figures are sufficiently clear to show that the sugar beet industry is no fly-by-night affair. It is not a hothouse concern, but a genuinely stable and economical industry.

Thank you. Surely, bearing in mind the three main considerations which I have outlined to-night, namely:
1.	That the sugar beet industry is economically sound in Canada;
2.	That the sugar beet industry is of very great value to agriculture as a whole in Canada;
3.	That the sugar beet industry is of great value in the whole economy of Canada;
Everyone throughout the country should be eager to do whatever is reasonably possible to cause the development of that industry within this dominion.
